From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 01:31:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] indexeddb: Read the records of an index

IDBIndex gets get(), getKey(), getAll(), getAllKeys() and count()
(https://w3c.github.io/IndexedDB/#index-interface), so the index records
that P3.1 and P3.2 store become readable. The methods follow their spec
steps: the index or its store has been deleted (InvalidStateError) is
checked before an inactive transaction (TransactionInactiveError), and
that before the key range conversion (DataError); the requests have the
index as their source.

Protocol: the store's read operations (GetKey, GetItem, GetAllKeys,
GetAllItems, Count) get an `index: Option<IndexedDBIndexId>`, the way
Chromium passes an index id to the same calls, instead of new variants.
GetKey of an index is "retrieve a value from an index", the primary key
of the first record; GetItem "retrieve a referenced value", the store's
value with its primary key, so that script injects a generated key
through IDBObjectStore::deserialize_value as for store reads.

Backend: the engine resolves the index id against the request's store
(a missing index fails only that request) and reads through one SQL
builder, `queries::RecordSource`, an object store or an index. An index
is read from its table (unique_index_data or index_data, P3.1) in
(index key, primary key) order, joined with object_data only when the
values are read; ranges apply to the index key, counts and limits as
for stores. The cursor read goes through the same builder with the store
as source, so index cursors (P3.4) and getAll's direction and records
(P4.1) only add columns and orderings. Reads queued behind a createIndex
population wait for it like any request (P3.2).

Script: getAll() and getAllKeys() of stores and indexes share
IDBRequest::retrieve_multiple_items, the spec's "create a request to
retrieve multiple items", with the query form only; P4.1 adds the
IDBGetAllOptions branch, the direction and the "record" kind there, and
the IDL keeps the spec's queryOrOptions and getAllRecords forms
commented. A count of 0 now gets every record, as the spec's "If count
is not given or is 0 (zero), let count be infinity" asks, also for
stores, which returned none.

The fork test index-reads.any.js also reads index keys that come from a
generated key back through the indexes (P3.1's placeholders): items of
a sequence key path and a multiEntry key path that are the store's key
path, a key path below it, which gives no key, and a nested store key
path whose key goes into the value's object or into one created for it.

Validation: `cargo nextest run -p servo-storage -p servo-storage-traits` (host SQLite): 137 passed, 1 skipped (P3.2 134/1), with index read tests on every executor (keys of every type in (index key, primary key) order, unique and multiEntry indexes, ranges, counts and a count of 0, a missing index failing alone; reads after puts, overwrites, deletes, clears and an abort; reads queued behind a three-batch createIndex population). Host WPT tests/wpt/tests/IndexedDB (4,511 subtests), 8 processes: SpiderMonkey 3,796, QuickJS 3,797 (previous commit 3,418 / 3,398, P3.2 tip 3,392 / 3,389); the 378 new passes over the previous commit, the same on both engines, are idbindex_get, _getKey, _getAll, _getAllKeys and _count, idbindex-query-exception-order, the method subtests of idbindex-request-source, idbindex-cross-realm-methods, idlharness's IDBIndex operations, reading-autoincrement-indexes, key-conversion-exceptions, idbindex-rename, -rename-errors and -rename-abort, idbindex-multientry, idbobjectstore_createIndex's index read subtests, idbobjectstore-rename-store, transaction-abort-index- and -multiple-metadata-revert, idb-partitioned-coverage, request-abort-ordering, idbfactory_open, idbdatabase_createObjectStore and the invalid-key subtests of idbindex_getAll(Keys)-options; expectations updated with `./mach update-wpt` from both engines' runs, after which SM ran 819 of 820 tests as expected (idbfactory-origin-isolation, a known flip) and QuickJS 820 of 820. tests/wpt/mozilla/tests/IndexedDB/ 200 of 200 on SM, QuickJS and SM --multiprocess, with the new index-reads.any.js (7 subtests in 4 globals; the P3.2 binary passes none). DAYU200 (QuickJS + RDB, 2 processes): WPT IndexedDB/ 3,773 (P3.2 3,381), the host's new passes except 10 of idb-partitioned-coverage, which now times out as a whole on the board (17 subtests): its iframe runs the index getAll tests, which takes 17 to 24 s on the board inside an iframe (4.7 s top-level, 0.65 s on the host), past the iframe harness's own 10 s timeout, and with that timeout disabled it completes in 24 s with only the two expected index cursor failures (RDB); the SQLite backend on the board times out the same way; the other unexpected results are the known board set (interleaved-cursors-large, idbfactory-origin-isolation, lone-surrogate rename subtests of worker/sharedworker variants, file_support's send_keys); /_mozilla/IndexedDB/ 200 of 200; `./mach test-unit --ohos -p servo-storage` RDB only 138 passed / 3 skipped, SQLite and RDB 245 / 4. `./mach clippy --locked -- -- --deny warnings`, `RUSTFLAGS="-D warnings" ./mach build --use-crown --locked --profile checked-release`, `./mach check --ohos -- -p servo-storage --all-targets` with SQLite, RDB and both, and test-tidy (except the known libsqlite3-sys advisory) pass. With the P3 review fixes folded in: `./mach check` without warnings and nextest 138 passed, 1 skipped at this commit; index-reads.any.js gets the generated-key subtest (8 subtests in 4 globals), which passes at the branch tip on SM, QuickJS and the DAYU200, and also with the P3.3 binary, as a regression test.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; implemented the index read protocol, queries and IDBIndex methods, wrote the unit and fork WPT tests, ran the host and board checks and updated the expectations; added the generated-key read-back subtest after the P3 review)
Signed-off-by: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
